 ```
 
+### Mixed notation
+
+`a[]` and `a[N]` mean different things: `a[]` appends to whatever the array holds so far, while `a[N]` places its value at index `N`. When a query string mixes both notations, explicit indices are always honoured in ascending order; if an explicit index lands on a slot that an earlier `a[]` already filled, both values are grouped together at that index rather than one of them being pushed to the end:
+
+```javascript
+var mixed = qs.parse('a[]=na&a[2]=2&a[0]=0&a[1]=1');
+assert.deepEqual(mixed, { a: [['na', '0'], '1', '2'] });
+
+// the result does not depend on the order of the parts
+var reordered = qs.parse('a[2]=2&a[]=na&a[0]=0&a[1]=1');
+assert.deepEqual(reordered, { a: [['na', '0'], '1', '2'] });
+
+// `a[]` after an explicit index simply appends, since nothing collides
+var appended = qs.parse('a[0]=0&a[]=na');
+assert.deepEqual(appended, { a: ['0', 'na'] });
+```
+
+This is the same grouping **qs** applies when the collection exceeds `arrayLimit` and becomes an object:
+
+```javascript
+var overflowed = qs.parse('a[]=na&a[20]=20&a[0]=0&a[10]=10');
+assert.deepEqual(overflowed, { a: { 0: ['na', '0'], 10: '10', 20: '20' } });
+```
+
 Note that an empty string is also a value, and will be preserved:
 
 ```javascript
